# mgburke.msg - Mister Burke (mgburke.ssl).
# 100 to 199 looks and floating lines, 200 and up conversation. One entry per line.

{100}{}{You see Mister Burke.}
{101}{}{A pressed blue suit, a pale hat and tinted spectacles, none of which have any business being this clean out here. He has watched you since you came in without once turning his head.}
{102}{}{Mister Burke stands well back from the camp fire, flicking ash from his sleeve and consulting a pocket watch.}

# 110 to 113: now and then, in his corner of the saloon.
{110}{}{Barman. This glass has a history. I should like one without.}
{111}{}{Do stop striking that radio. It has suffered enough.}
{112}{}{Charming. One can taste the rust.}
{113}{}{One was told this would be a short posting.}

# 120 answering the arrest, 121 after shooting Simms, 122 turning on the player, 123 the charge was
# pulled out again, 124 leaving, 125 spoken to while hostile, 126 while leaving, 127 during the
# scene, 128 the charge is fitted but he has not left his table yet.
{120}{}{Sheriff. There is no need for theater. I shall come quietly.}
{121}{}{A pity. He had such presence.}
{122}{}{How very tiresome.}
{123}{}{You have cost my employer a view. He will have the difference in blood.}
{124}{}{Good day.}
{125}{}{We have nothing further to discuss.}
{126}{}{Do keep walking.}
{127}{}{Presently.}
{128}{}{Outside. By the fire. I do not repeat myself.}
# 129, 131: the message window when the sheriff shoots him first, seen from inside the saloon / heard from elsewhere in town.
{129}{}{The sheriff's rifle cracks once. Burke sits down hard, looks at his shirt front with real annoyance, and slides off the chair.}
{131}{}{Up at the saloon a rifle cracks, once. Then the sheriff's voice, level, asking somebody for a blanket.}
# 130: caught the player's hand in his pocket.
{130}{}{That pocket is spoken for. Do not make me say so twice.}

# The pitch.
{200}{}{Ah. A new face. Do sit. My name is Burke. I represent a gentleman who finds this town an offense to the eye. I have a device that would let its bomb finish what it came for. I lack only steady hands and an absence of sentiment.}
{201}{}{Why would anyone want a town gone?}
{202}{}{How much?}
{203}{}{I'm telling the sheriff about this.}
{204}{}{Leave Megaton. Today.}
{205}{}{Boom? Me like boom!}
{206}{}{Not interested.}
{207}{}{The sheriff knows what you're selling. Leave while you can walk.}
{210}{}{My employer, Mister Tenpenny, is raising a tower for people of quality. From its balcony one may see for miles, and for miles in every direction one sees this. A boil. He would like it lanced. The crater may stay. Craters are picturesque.}
{220}{}{One thousand dollars, in coin, when the horizon is tidy. More money than this town will see in ten years, for an afternoon's work.}
{221}{}{Fifteen hundred, or find other hands.}
{222}{}{Something up front. Call it good faith.}
{223}{}{Deal.}
{224}{}{No.}
{225}{}{...Fifteen hundred. You know your own value. I do so prefer that to gratitude.}
{226}{}{Faith. How quaint. Two hundred and fifty now, deducted from the balance. And this: a fusion pulse charge. Fit it to the socket behind the bomb's access plate, then find me outside the gate, by the caravan fire.}

# The same for a player of very few words.
{230}{}{...I see. Yes. Boom. Listen closely. I give you a toy. You put the toy inside the big metal ball. Then you come to me, outside, by the fire. Then: boom. Then: many shiny coins. Yes?}
{231}{}{Me do! Give toy!}
{232}{}{No. Boom ball scary.}
{233}{}{Me go.}
{234}{}{Splendid. Here is the toy. Inside the big metal ball. Not in your mouth. Then outside, by the fire.}
{235}{}{Toy! Me go put!}
{236}{}{Me lose toy.}

{240}{}{Splendid. This is a fusion pulse charge. Fit it to the socket behind the bomb's access plate and walk away. You will find me outside the gate, by the caravan fire. I prefer to watch from a distance.}
{241}{}{What if I can't get it to fit?}
{242}{}{I'll see you outside.}
{245}{}{Then improve yourself. The shop girl had the thing open once and wrote it all down, and the town doctor keeps pills that sharpen the wits. I am told you people can be resourceful.}
{250}{}{A conscience. How rustic. The offer stands for as long as I can bear this room, which will not be long.}
{251}{}{[Leave.]}
{255}{}{Reconsidered? The offer has not improved. Then again, neither has the town.}
{260}{}{Do. He will find a traveler with a paid tab and nothing in his pockets but a handkerchief. And I shall remember which of us made the afternoon unpleasant. Good day.}

# Leaving town: 270 talked into it, 272 told that the sheriff knows.
{270}{}{...One weighs these things. A town of scrap against an afternoon of unpleasantness with you. Very well. Megaton may keep its boil a while longer. You, I think, will not keep much.}
{271}{}{[Watch him go.]}
{272}{}{Does he. Then this room has become crowded. You have a gift for closing doors, my friend. My employer has a gift for remembering who closed them.}

# The job is taken and the bomb untouched.
{280}{}{You are still here, and so is the town. I had hoped to be disappointed by only one of you.}
{281}{}{I lost the charge.}
{283}{}{I've changed my mind. Take it back.}
{284}{}{I'm working on it.}
{285}{}{Lost. A fusion pulse charge. ...Here. Its price comes out of your patience, not your fee, because I am a generous man. Do not test how generous.}
{286}{}{Again. I begin to suspect you of selling them. Here. If this one goes astray, fit yourself to the socket.}
{287}{}{How disappointing. The charge, if you please. ...Thank you. Should your principles tire you, you know which corner I keep.}

# The bomb was disarmed under his nose.
{290}{}{The bomb is silent, I hear, and by your hand. You have made an enemy of a man who can see three counties from his balcony. He has a long memory. I keep his lists.}
{291}{}{Then get out of my sight.}
{292}{}{Suit man go now.}

# At the rendezvous.
{300}{}{Is it done? ...Yes. One can almost hear it humming from here. Splendid. Shall we?}
{301}{}{Do it.}
{302}{}{I've changed my mind.}
{303}{}{Give me a moment.}
{305}{}{Do not dawdle. The light is perfect.}
{310}{}{No. You have changed your price, and I do not renegotiate at the door.}
{311}{}{[He reaches inside his jacket.]}
{320}{}{Burke opens a slim case and turns a key no bigger than a tooth. For a moment there is only the wind. Then the horizon turns white, and a second later the ground arrives.}
{321}{}{[Hold on to something.]}
{322}{}{Magnificent. Mister Tenpenny will be so pleased with the view. Your fee, as agreed. Our business is concluded. Do keep walking, there's a good fellow. The wind will turn.}
{323}{}{[Take the money.]}

# The message window, while the dust is still going up. 342 + amount + 343.
{340}{}{Where Megaton stood, a column of dirty light climbs into the sky and slowly forgets what shape it meant to be.}
{341}{}{The wall, the saloon, the sheriff's hat: none of it comes down. There is nothing left to come down.}
{342}{}{Burke counts }
{343}{}{ dollars into your hand without looking at you.}
{344}{}{You have destroyed Megaton. The road is all there is now.}
